#{[17592186348008 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186345393 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348008 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186345747 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348008 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186346261 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348008 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186346400 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348008 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186347073 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348008 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348008 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348008 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186346400 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348008 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e67277c2-ffd2-5937-8d1e-923e1b9e37f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348266 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347742 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348266 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79740" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348266 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348266 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the differential $\\text{d}f$ for the function $f(x,y)=4{y}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348266 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "08d66e91-862a-530e-b18f-86f79ced0d5f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348266 #edb/ref 1074 "We compute the differential using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{4{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x}{x}+12{y}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348267 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347697 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348267 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79741" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348267 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348267 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the differential $\\text{d}f$ for the function $f(x,y)={e}^{xy}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348267 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d50fccec-ae2d-5c4e-8f8a-368a11f02916" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348267 #edb/ref 1074 "We compute the differential using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =y{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}x+x{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348270 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347603 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348270 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79746" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348270 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348270 #edb/ref 1071 "Given a rectangular solid, find the differential $\\text{d}V$ for the volume $V$ of the rectangular solid if the lengths of the sides are $a=2,$ $b=5,$ and $c=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348270 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "eb226681-0351-549f-b81b-c5875e8a6018" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348270 #edb/ref 1074 "The volume of the rectangular solid is $V=abc.$ Let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}V(a,b,c) & =(\\frac{∂V}{∂a})\\,\\text{d}a+(\\frac{∂V}{∂b})\\,\\text{d}b+(\\frac{∂V}{∂c})\\,\\text{d}c \\\\  & =bc\\,\\text{d}a+ac\\,\\text{d}b+ab\\,\\text{d}c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $a=2,$ $b=5,$ and $c=1,$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}V(2,5,1) & =5\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}a+2\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}b+5\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}c \\\\  & =5\\,\\text{d}a+2\\,\\text{d}b+10\\,\\text{d}c.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348287 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347769 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348287 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79809" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348287 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348287 #edb/ref 1071 "If $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,$ use the differential $\\text{d}f$ to estimate the change $Δf$ in the value of $f(x,y)$ from $(2,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ to $(1.5,1.5).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348287 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "660c5793-fb22-5668-8662-5f26917785f7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348287 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}x+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(2,\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(2,\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{d}x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =1\\,\\text{d}x+0\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(2+Δx,\\frac{\\pi }{2}+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈1\\,Δx+0\\,Δy.\n$$\n\nMoving from $(2,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ to $(1.5,1.5),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =1.5-2=-0.5, \\\\ Δy & =1.5-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈1\\,Δx+0\\,Δy \\\\  & ≈1\\cdot (-0.5)+0\\cdot (1.5-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & ≈-0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $f(x,y)$ decreases by approximately $0.5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348289 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347860 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348289 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79810" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348289 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348289 #edb/ref 1071 "If $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y+\\sqrt{y},$ use the differential $\\text{d}f$ to estimate the change $Δf$ in the value of $f(x,y)$ from $(0,4)$ to $(-1,5).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348289 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b3a7a619-a349-551d-ae22-16b086347f4a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348289 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2xy\\,\\text{d}x+({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}})\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(0,4),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(0,4) & =2\\cdot 0\\cdot 4\\,\\text{d}x+({0}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{4}})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =0\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(0+Δx,4+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈0\\,Δx+\\frac{1}{4}\\,Δy.\n$$\n\nMoving from $(0,4)$ to $(-1,5),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =-1-0=-1, \\\\ Δy & =5-4=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈0\\,Δx+\\frac{1}{4}\\,Δy \\\\  & ≈0\\cdot (-1)+\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (1) \\\\  & ≈0.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $f(x,y)$ increases by approximately $0.25.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348304 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347950 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348304 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79842" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348304 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348304 #edb/ref 1071 "Estimate $2.1{e}^{0.2}$ using a differential $\\text{d}f$ of the function $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y}$ at the point $(2,0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348304 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1639694c-cbca-54ad-bf88-2314edcecfd3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348304 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}x+x{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(2,0),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & ={e}^{0}\\,\\text{d}x+2\\cdot {e}^{0}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =1\\,\\text{d}x+2\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(2+Δx,0+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈1\\,Δx+2\\,Δy\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n2.1{e}^{0.2}=f(2.1,0.2),\n$$\n\nso we want to move to the point $(2.1,0.2).$\n\nMoving from $(2,0)$ to $(2.1,0.2),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =2.1-2=0.1, \\\\ Δy & =0.2-0=0.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈1\\cdot (0.1)+2\\cdot (0.2) \\\\  & ≈0.1+0.4 \\\\  & ≈0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that the value of $f$ decreases by about $0.5.$ Consequently, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2.1,0.2) & ≈f(2,0)+0.5 \\\\ 2.1{e}^{0.2} & ≈2{e}^{0}+0.5 \\\\  & ≈2+0.5 \\\\  & ≈2.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348321 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347902 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348321 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79958" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348321 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348321 #edb/ref 1071 "Given a right cone, find the differential $\\text{d}l$ for the slant height $l$ of the right cone if its base radius is $r=3$ and height $h=4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348321 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "00fbdc66-0e7c-5f09-b062-4832cba6efd9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348321 #edb/ref 1074 "The slant height of the right cone is $l=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{h}^{2}}.$ Let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}l(r,h) & =(\\frac{∂l}{∂r})\\,\\text{d}r+(\\frac{∂l}{∂h})\\,\\text{d}h \\\\  & =\\frac{r}{\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{h}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}r+\\frac{h}{\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{h}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}h\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $r=3$  and $h=4$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}V(3,4) & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}r+\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}h \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\,\\text{d}r+\\frac{4}{5}\\,\\text{d}h.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351615 #edb/ref 1080 "0.6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351615 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351615 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "055e28da-53a3-56a0-936c-0d9671fd3eee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351850 #edb/ref 1080 "-2.6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351850 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351850 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2002afe9-57a6-5eea-89b1-769b51a8d9f8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186352147 #edb/ref 1080 "-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186352147 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186352147 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3e7b4730-07bc-55e3-a6a1-1f8f741572c1" #edb/ref 175921860