[17592186354194 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186353712 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354194 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354194 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354194 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186352631 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354194 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "de653567-a501-57e8-a380-cad8d0781232" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354256 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186352147 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354256 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186352706 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354256 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186352953 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354256 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186353083 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354256 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186353179 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354256 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354256 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354256 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186352147 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354256 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fb9582cd-856c-58b5-9f89-f922a1fb2623" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354501 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354256 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354501 #edb/ref 1069 "q-86715" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354501 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354501 #edb/ref 1071 "If $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡({z}^{3}-y),$ use the differential $\\text{d}f$ to estimate the change $Δf$ in the value of $f(x,y,z)$ from $(2,-3,1)$ to $(1,-2,2).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354501 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d42b8603-2969-573d-b945-d7e1061c7be0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354501 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y,z) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y+(\\frac{∂f}{∂z})\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2x\\,\\text{d}x-(\\frac{4}{{z}^{3}-y})\\,\\text{d}y+(\\frac{12{z}^{2}}{{z}^{3}-y})\\,\\text{d}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(2,-3,1),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(2,-3,1) & =(2\\cdot 2)\\,\\text{d}x-(\\frac{4}{{1}^{3}-(-3)})\\,\\text{d}y+(\\frac{12\\cdot {1}^{2}}{{1}^{3}-(-3)})\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =4\\,\\text{d}x-\\,\\text{d}y+3\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(2+Δx,-3+Δy,1+Δz),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈4\\,Δx-\\,Δy+3\\,Δz.\n$$\n\nMoving from $(2,-3,1)$ to $(1,-2,2),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =1-2=-1, \\\\ Δy & =-2-(-3)=1 \\\\ Δz & =2-1=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈4\\,Δx-1\\,Δy+3\\,Δz \\\\  & ≈4\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ≈-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $f(x,y,z)$ decreases by approximately $-2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354503 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354194 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354503 #edb/ref 1069 "q-86717" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354503 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354503 #edb/ref 1071 "Estimate ${e}^{(0.3)(2.15)}$ using a differential $\\text{d}f$ of the function $f(x,y)={e}^{xy}$ at the point $(0,2).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354503 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cb4b46a5-3d8b-55b1-a7c8-ab6f3feb209f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354503 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =y{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}x+x{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(0,2),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =2\\cdot {e}^{0\\cdot 2}\\,\\text{d}x+0\\cdot {e}^{0\\cdot 2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\,\\text{d}x+0\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(0+Δx,2+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈2\\,Δx+0\\,Δy.\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n{e}^{(0.3)(2.15)}=f(0.3,2.15),\n$$\n\nso we want to move to the point $(0.3,2.15).$\n\nMoving from $(0,2)$ to $(0.3,2.15),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =0.3-0=0.3, \\\\ Δy & =2.15-2=0.15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈2\\cdot (0.3)+0\\cdot (0.15) \\\\  & ≈0.6\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that the value of $f$ increases by about $0.6.$ Consequently, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0.3,2.15) & ≈f(0,2)+0.6 \\\\ {e}^{(0.3)(2.15)} & ≈{e}^{(0)(2)}+0.6 \\\\  & ≈1+0.6 \\\\  & ≈1.6\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372349 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186372350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372349 #edb/ref 1069 "q-168149" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372349 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372349 #edb/ref 1071 "Given a right triangular prism, find the differential $\\text{d}V$ for the volume $V$ if the height is $h=5$ and the base is a right triangle with legs $a=4$ and $b=6.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372349 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8ad2cde7-ba79-5b5e-a983-25dd72f62433" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372349 #edb/ref 1074 "The volume of a given right triangular prism is $V=\\frac{abh}{2}.$ Let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}V(a,b,c) & =(\\frac{∂V}{∂a})\\,\\text{d}a+(\\frac{∂V}{∂b})\\,\\text{d}b+(\\frac{∂V}{∂h})\\,\\text{d}h \\\\  & =\\frac{bh}{2}\\,\\text{d}a+\\frac{ah}{2}\\,\\text{d}b+\\frac{ab}{2}\\,\\text{d}h\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $a=4,$ $b=6,$ and $h=5,$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}V(4,6,5) & =\\frac{6\\cdot 5}{2}\\,\\text{d}a+\\frac{4\\cdot 5}{2}\\,\\text{d}b+\\frac{4\\cdot 6}{2}\\,\\text{d}h \\\\  & =15\\,\\text{d}a+10\\text{d}b+12\\,\\text{d}h.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372350 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372351 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372350 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372352 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372350 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372353 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372350 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372354 #edb/ref 17592186045